      "before_assertion": {
        "method": "LABEL_ENUMERATION",
        "completeness": "PARTIAL",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "evidence_type": "RECIPE_SOURCE",
            "source": "https://fsl.nmsu.edu/documents/difcobblmanual_2nded_lowres.pdf",
            "source_record": "BD Difco & BBL Manual, Second Edition (2009), Columbia Agars, printed page158 / PDF page170",
            "notes": "Recovered manufacturer-authored BD Difco & BBL Manual, second edition 2009, printed p158 (PDF page170), hosted by NMSU. Original capture 2026-11-08T06:55:09.048187+00:00, SHA256 e5e836a75f13283d477467b20ffc0d332531e0f3a0effb5d64a7147a6985c0b7. The approximate per-liter Difco table lists pancreatic digest of casein10g, Proteose Peptone No.3 5g, yeast extract5g, beef heart infusion from500g 3g, corn starch1g, sodium chloride5g and agar15g; the footnote allows adjustment/supplementation to meet performance criteria. It directs suspension of44g powder in1L purified water. Those are manufacturer preparation/formula statements, not dry-product mass fractions, final yield, or an identified historical JCM lot. The same page separately gives BBL42.5g (including13.5g agar, meat peptic digest and heart pancreatic digest) and DifcoEH39g (including12g agar and different nutrient inputs). The older mirrored manual has different heart wording and BBL proportions and is retained as separate version context. The old search wrapper and failed official/mirror requests remain historical leads, not primary manual evidence."
          }
        ],
        "notes": "Seven named constituents only, scoped to the nominal 2009 Difco manufacturer formulation. PARTIAL is retained for the source-qualified JCM1270 input: exact historical lot and any performance-related adjustments are unverified. No blood or reconstitution water is a dry-base component."
      },
      "after_assertion": {
        "method": "CURATED_INTERPRETATION",
        "completeness": "COMPLETE",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "evidence_type": "RECIPE_SOURCE",
            "source": "https://fsl.nmsu.edu/documents/difcobblmanual_2nded_lowres.pdf",
            "source_record": "BD Difco & BBL Manual, Second Edition (2009), Columbia Agars, printed page158 / PDF page170",
            "notes": "Recovered manufacturer-authored BD Difco & BBL Manual, second edition 2009, printed p158 (PDF page170), hosted by NMSU. Original capture 2026-11-08T06:55:09.048187+00:00, SHA256 e5e836a75f13283d477467b20ffc0d332531e0f3a0effb5d64a7147a6985c0b7. The approximate per-liter Difco table lists pancreatic digest of casein10g, Proteose Peptone No.3 5g, yeast extract5g, beef heart infusion from500g 3g, corn starch1g, sodium chloride5g and agar15g; the footnote allows adjustment/supplementation to meet performance criteria. It directs suspension of44g powder in1L purified water. Those are manufacturer preparation/formula statements, not dry-product mass fractions, final yield, or an identified historical JCM lot. The same page separately gives BBL42.5g (including13.5g agar, meat peptic digest and heart pancreatic digest) and DifcoEH39g (including12g agar and different nutrient inputs). The older mirrored manual has different heart wording and BBL proportions and is retained as separate version context. The old search wrapper and failed official/mirror requests remain historical leads, not primary manual evidence."
          }
        ],
        "notes": "CURATED_INTERPRETATION covers the seven immediate ingredient names in the nominal Difco Columbia Blood Agar Base formula published in the BD Difco & BBL Manual, second edition (2009), printed page 158 / PDF page 170. COMPLETE applies only to that nominal constituent-name list: all seven listed immediate ingredients are represented. This is not a complete molecular composition, an exact historical JCM1270 lot formulation, or a quantitative recipe transcription. The manufacturer performance-adjustment/supplementation footnote and unknown actual JCM lot remain explicit limitations. Component quantities and dry-mass ratios are not asserted. Blood and reconstitution water are not dry-base components. Existing mixed-owner partition and owner/role/export holds remain."
      },
